\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Divergence and the discrete Poisson equation}
\date{2026-11-08}


\p{The matrix #{B} of \ref{fdip-0003} is the [incidence matrix](fdip-00AF),
and #{L=-B^{\mathsf T}B} is the graph Laplacian. The matrix
#{-B^{\mathsf T}} is the discrete divergence.
In the electrostatic setting, the edge field #{\mathcal E=-B\varphi}
is the discrete counterpart of the continuum field #{E} of
[the field equations in three languages](fdip-00AA).
Gauss's law #{\nabla\cdot\mathcal E=\rho} becomes
##{\,(-B^{\mathsf T})(-B\varphi)=B^{\mathsf T}B\varphi=-L\varphi=\rho,}
the discrete Poisson equation for this gradient field. Computations,
and the later Laplacian chapters, use #{d} in this matrix form.}
